nd me  trying to get on either evacuation  trains or simply buy a ticket  on in any empty seat to get further east  to safety these people have been coming  from  the largely eastern parts of the country  and south  east but of course tensions are very  high here as you have been reporting  this morning about that uh attack on the  kramatorsk train station that's about  200 kilometers east from where i'm  standing and obviously that has  heightened tension everyone here is  worried that that's going to happen in  fact we've been able to film inside the  station on the platform or anyone  getting onto any trains because  authorities are really worried that  something like that could happen here  i mean how are these people who arrive  there i mean surely they must be  traumatized very weary certainly how are  they being looked after before they move  on  well there's a shelter that's been set  up here it's being supported by the red  cross largely run by local authorities  excuse me there's just a tram going  behind me so i hope you can hear me  so that shelter is there for people who  are arriving here they can stay a couple  of nights or a night uh have you know  some refreshments have a hot meal get  some clothes and then sort of move and  then move on that's why the the trains  that leave in the afternoon the  station's starting to fill up because  the people come out of those shelters  and try and get onto those trains i  actually spoke to somebody who's been  volunteering at that stage at that  shelter since the beginning uh since  it's been open from early march and i  asked her how she felt working in the  aftermath of the kramer taurts attack  and whether or not they're worried about  something happening here this is what  she had to say  it was not there  you know if i imagine that situation  happening here  i wouldn't be able to work here  and create the calm space and support  these people need  a place where they can sleep without the  sound of explosions  no  rebecca because you're there and you one  can tell heightened alert and people uh  are still holding their grounds trying  to help those that need help  um  will you stay put  um well where and where not where  actually we'll be leaving a lot of  people here though have said that they  are going to stay like like um  like the woman i spoke to it from the  shelter  these people don't necessarily want to  leave their homes unless they feel it's  absolutely necessary lots of people here  in nepal say they're going to stay here  stay around they don't feel that it's  too dangerous yet but as we've seen in  other cities too too too late it can be  really dangerous as well so definitely  uh people the the refugees that have  been arriving here will be moving on but  a lot of people still sticking around  here in nepro all right uh rebecca thank  you so much for your reporting and to  stay safe  you
 

